by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T12:29:21Z

Thanks for your patience, and for applying the earlier requests.

First the good news, because it is the part that matters most for a debug command: the routing resolution is right. I ran eleven message classes through the command and then through a real `dispatch()` against in-memory transports, in two configurations, one with a `*` route and one without. All twenty-two comparisons matched: subclass inheriting the parent route, interface route, two transports for one message, unrouted-but-handled falling back to `*`, namespace wildcard beating `*`, config beating `#[AsMessage]`, `*` beating `#[AsMessage]`, attribute inherited from a parent class, and multi-transport attributes. The command does not lie, which is the hard part.

What I would like to change is the packaging. I think this belongs inside `debug:messenger` rather than in a second command.

The per-message mode is the genuinely new capability. Nothing today resolves a concrete FQCN through parents, interfaces, wildcard precedence and the attribute fallback: `debug:container --tag messenger.message` prints nothing because resource tags are excluded, and `debug:container messenger.senders_locator` shows only `Array (5 element(s))`. That is worth having. But `debug:messenger` already loops bus, then message, then handlers, so the row to hang it off already exists:

```
App\BaseMessage
    handled by App\Handler
    routed to async
```

That answers "what happens when I dispatch this" in one place, which is the question people actually have.

For the reverse question, which transports carry what, I would keep a listing but render it as rules rather than as a flat list of map keys. As it stands the listing prints `App\Marker` and a bare `*` with no indication of what they are, and a reader cannot tell an interface from a class from a fallback:

```
Transports
----------
async
    App\BaseMessage
    App\Foo\*        (namespace)
    App\Marker       (interface, matches implementors)
failed
    *               (fallback for messages with no other route)
```

`debug:config framework messenger.routing` does print the same map, but it is not discoverable and it explains none of this, so I would rather have it here.

One structural detail if you take this on: `routing` is configured as a sibling of `buses` under `framework.messenger`, not per bus. So the transports section has to be printed once, outside the `foreach ($mapping as $bus => ...)` loop, otherwise the output implies routing varies per bus.

For the deep view I would use an option rather than a second argument, since `bus` already occupies the first slot and detection would be ambiguous:

```
php bin/console debug:messenger --message='App\Message\FooMessage'
```

And whatever we print, it should say that `TransportNamesStamp` can override routing at dispatch time. No static view can see that, so anyone using `RedispatchMessage` would otherwise read the output as authoritative when it is not.

Three things that need fixing regardless of the packaging:

- The Messenger CHANGELOG entry is in the released `7.4` section. Messenger has no `8.2` section yet, so one needs creating. The FrameworkBundle entry is correctly placed.
- The low-deps failure is caused by the PR. `FrameworkExtensionTestCase::testAsMessageAutoconfigurationUsesResourceTag` calls `new AsMessage(transport: ..., serializedTypeName: ...)`, but FrameworkBundle allows `symfony/messenger ^7.4.10|^8.0.10`, where that parameter does not exist, so it fails with `Unknown named parameter $serializedTypeName`.
- On rebase, take care with the `addResourceTag()` one-liner. Base now also tags `serializedTypeNameAliases`, and a naive resolution drops it.

Smaller ones: `complete()` offers `*` and namespace wildcards as filter values, but choosing `*` then throws "class does not exist"; the private `displayMessageRouting()` sits before the public `complete()`, and we put private methods last; a transport with no routes renders a full warning block; and the `Note:` line repeats on every run.

One idea I am deliberately leaving out of scope, in case you like it as a follow-up. `SendersLocator::getSenders()` owns the real precedence but interleaves "which aliases apply" with "fetch the sender from the locator". If the alias half were split into its own method, the command could call the same code the runtime calls, and the two could not drift. Your implementation agrees with it today, so this is an improvement rather than a fix, and it does not need to block this PR.

Happy to do the fold myself if you would rather not, just say so.

